An AVL Tree supports three primary operations: Search, Insertion, and Deletion. Because the tree maintains strict balance, every operation runs in O(log N) time.

Below is a breakdown of how each operation works under the hood.

1. Search Operation

Searching in an AVL tree works identically to a standard Binary Search Tree (BST).

Start at the root node.
If the target value equals the current node's key, you're done.
If the target value is smaller, move to the left child.
If the target value is larger, move to the right child.
Repeat until you find the value or hit a null reference.

Time Complexity: O(log N) worst-case. Because the height H is strictly bounded by 1.44 log2(N), searching is faster here than in almost any other BST variant.

2. Insertion Operation

Insertion follows a standard BST approach, but backtracks up the tree to update heights and restore balance if needed.

Standard BST Insert: Recursively navigate down to a leaf position and attach the new node.
Update Heights: On the way back up the call stack, update the height of every ancestor node.
Calculate Balance Factor (BF): For each ancestor, check BF = Height(Left) - Height(Right).
Rebalance (if |BF| > 1): Identify the imbalance type and apply the appropriate rotation:


Case 1 (LL): BF > 1 and new node was inserted in left child's left subtree -> Single Right Rotation.
Case 2 (RR): BF < -1 and new node was inserted in right child's right subtree -> Single Left Rotation.
Case 3 (LR): BF > 1 and new node was inserted in left child's right subtree -> Left Rotation on left child, then Right Rotation on root.
Case 4 (RL): BF < -1 and new node was inserted in right child's left subtree -> Right Rotation on right child, then Left Rotation on root.

Note: At most one or two rotations are ever required to rebalance the entire tree after an insertion.

3. Deletion Operation

Deletion is slightly more complex because removing a node can trigger multiple rebalances up the tree to the root.

Standard BST Delete:


0 children (Leaf): Simply remove the node.
1 child: Replace the node with its child.
2 children: Find the Inorder Successor (smallest node in the right subtree) or Inorder Predecessor (largest node in the left subtree), copy its value into the target node, and delete that successor/predecessor instead.
Update Heights & Check BF: Backtrack toward the root, updating node heights.
Rebalance: If a node becomes unbalanced (|BF| > 1), perform rotations similar to insertion. Unlike insertion, removing a node can cause cascade imbalances, so you may need up to O(log N) rotations along the path to the root.

Summary of Operation Complexities

| Operation | Time Complexity | Auxiliary Space | Max Rotations Required |
| ---       | ---              | ---            | ---                    |
| Search    |   O(log N)       |      O(1)      |          0             |
| Insertion |    O(log N)      |    O(log N)    |      At most 2         |
| Deletion  |    O(log N)      |    O(log N)    |    Up to O(log N)      |

